foreign  [Music]  news briefing for Tuesday October 25th  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal  the chip industry is in a period of  transition Intel which reports earnings  later this week has been hit hard by a  recent softening of demand for  semiconductors but the company has also  had some recent wins including the  passage of the bipartisan chips act  that'll pour about 52 billion dollars  into expanding domestic production of  chips and Intel is betting big on U.S  production with plans to build foundries  in Ohio and Arizona as Western companies  look to break their Reliance on Asian  Supply chains Intel CEO Patrick  gelsinger kicked off our Tech live  conference yesterday speaking about this  and more with wsj editor Thorold Barker  here are highlights from their  conversation hello everybody and Pat  welcome to Tech live thanks very much  for coming to be our first Speaker we've  got a lot of ground to cover so for the  last few years we've read endlessly  about the shortage of chips in the world  and not be able to get enough for cars  and other things that seems to have now  flipped into a different situation where  there's an excess of chips and prices  have come down and you've had some  pretty tough tough earnings you've been  in the industry a long time is this a  chip cycle or is this an economic  recession that you're looking at well  obviously chips are super important to  the planet right you know it's why we  saw the chips act why we saw you know  all this discussion around uh chips uh  broadly and you know the expectation is  by the end of the decade the chips  industry approximately doubles you know  today about 600 billion by the end of  the decade 1.1 trillion right in that  view so you clearly have a growing  industry long term so fundamentally we  have a long-term growth cycle in front  of us and we have near-term wild Cycles  right obviously you know the  covid-induced cycle where sort of  overnight right we saw Supply chains  disrupted so Supply decreased by  five-ish percent on an industry that  typically is growing by five percent per  year five six percent per year and  demand went to 25 percent per year so  all of a sudden you see this huge  acceleration and demand right decrease  in Supply disruptions and Supply chains  right and I'll just say the over  stimulation of the economies around the  world and clearly that was most  accentuated in the semiconductor  industry well now we're catching up  right you've seen the economy slow down  around the world Supply chains for the  first time you know so it really is one  of those Cycles but it's against the  backdrop where the industry is going to  double this decade so we have to keep  building those long-term Factory build  outs even though we have to make  near-term adjustments and price  variations right in dealing with you  know Shanghai port shutdowns and so on  it's been a pretty dramatic you know a  year and a half so you've been in  Washington a lot helping to push through  the chips act how Reliant is Intel now  on subsidy going forward  first let's contextualize the chips act  right and we have the U.S chips Act and  the EU chips act you know if we were  building Manufacturing in Asia you know  the expectations and Sia estimates say  it'd be 30 or 40 percent cheaper if we  were building in Asia Supply chains are  now firmly Consolidated there it is  simply economically uncompetitive to  build in U.S or Europe and that's why we  saw the industry move from 80 20 in 1990  in U.S and Europe to 2080 today you know  it's been a dramatic shift over the last  30 years so the chips Act was balancing  you know essentially leveling that  playing field so your strategy would  still have been the same in terms of  both the design and the production of  chips you'd have just been doing it  somewhere else absolutely it has to be  competitive when you're moving that  level of investment right got to be  competitive so a lot of this is about  China can you talk a little bit about  what your view is on the latest rules  out of the byte Administration to stop  technology transfer both in terms of the  technology itself and the people doing  that what is your take on this is this a  good thing I think it was inevitable  right you know when you know this view  of how something so critical to every  industry every business and are we going  to you know be able to continue to have  leadership on those Technologies part of  the reason I've been such an advocate  for many of these policies I viewed this  geopolitically as inevitable and that's  why the rebalancing of Supply chains it  was so critical right you know are we  going to have the supply chains that we  need for our Autos you know for our AI  for our national security for game  consoles for our PCS when you were  loving for the chips that were you also  pushing for this no no but you're  welcoming of it yeah I you know clearly  expected this uh to be the case it was  part of the reason that I've always used  the phrase geographically balanced  resilient Supply chains you know Taiwan  plays such a critical role you know for  the technology Supply chains but it's  precarious even the Taiwanese vendors  believe deeply they need their supply  chains to become more balanced so you  know I view this very inevitable right  as we look to the Future and given that  you know none of the policies that are  you know being put in place by I don't  think any of us would be surprised by  that and just quickly what is the gap  like now between Chinese capability and  Western capability yeah today you know  the most advanced in China right is  about 14 nanometers uh today you know  different different estimates uh today  you know we're doing uh seven nanometers  about to do four right tsmc and taiwan's  about to start their three production  and you know we're racing to have our  sub two nanometers by 2025. you know  when the policies that you've seen are  basically to maintain or increase that  gap between where China is all right and  where U.S or european capabilities uh  would be great thank you very much Pat  all right that's it for today's Tech  news briefing Tech live continues  tomorrow for live coverage of the event  visit our website wsj.com and journal  subscribers can get a complimentary  online pass at wsj.com Tech live I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

